% ===========================================================================
%  YOUR REFERENCES.  Add entries here; cite them with \cite{key}, where key is
%  the first field of the entry (e.g. upper1974).
%
%  WHERE ENTRIES COME FROM.  You rarely type one by hand.  Most journal sites
%  and databases have a "Cite" or "Export citation" button that offers BibTeX;
%  copy what it gives you and paste it below.  Google Scholar does the same.
%  If you keep a library in Zotero, Mendeley or EndNote, export the whole
%  library as a .bib file and either replace this file with it or paste the
%  entries in.  Check what you paste: exported entries are often wrong about
%  capitalisation, page ranges and author initials.
%
%  CITATION STYLE.  This template cites and formats the bibliography in IEEE
%  numeric style, set by the bibstyle and citestyle options on line 720 of
%  ttuthesis2026.cls.  Another style -- APA, Chicago author-year, and so on --
%  means changing those two biblatex options; it is not automated here, but
%  biblatex is well documented and a search for "biblatex <style name>" will
%  name the option to use.  The Graduate School manual does not pick a style
%  for you: it defers to your department's approved style guide (p.5), so ask
%  before you change anything.
%
%  An entry may carry both journal and shortjournal. Which one is printed is
%  decided by journal-abbreviations in config/thesis-config.tex. DOIs are
%  recorded here but never rendered, so keep them: they cost nothing and make
%  the entry easy to verify later.
% ===========================================================================

